From 86c9fbb0fc4086bcfc411ed9eb3d88a5d8131250 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Book Buddy Dev <dev@bookbuddy.local>
Date: Sat, 18 Jul 2026 04:09:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: author collectible cards on book finish + binder page

Adds a lightweight trading-card layer on top of the existing badge/title
rarity pattern (see 012_events_badges_admin.sql):

- 014_author_cards.sql: author_cards catalog (code, fun fact, an invented
  'memorabilia' flavor item, icon, rarity) + user_author_cards collection
  (quantity + per-copy serial codes, stacking dupes instead of duplicate
  rows). RLS lets a signed-in kid insert/update their own rows directly,
  unlike badges/titles which are admin/cron-only -- new authors are
  discovered live, inside a normal user request. Seeds 8 classic authors
  at rare/legendary; everything else is created at common on first finish.

- lib/author-cards.ts: getOrCreateAuthorCard() (Gemini-generated fun
  fact + artifact flavor with a fail-open generic fallback), and
  awardAuthorCardForFinishedBook() which rolls a drop -- guaranteed on a
  kid's first book by a given author, guaranteed while any weekend event
  is active, otherwise an 18% baseline (CARD_DROP_RATE_BASE).

- app/api/reading/log/route.ts: hooks the roll in right after the
  existing finish-book/XP/event-progress flow, wrapped in try/catch so a
  card-system failure can never block XP, streaks, or challenge results.

- app/kids/collection + components/kids/CollectionClient.tsx: new
  'binder' page listing every catalog card, owned ones shown with icon/
  fun fact/artifact/serial codes and a x-count for dupes, unowned ones as
  silhouettes -- classic collect-them-all completion pull. Linked from
  KidsNav as 'Cards'.

- components/kids/ShelfClient.tsx: reveal modal on the existing
  finish-book flow, with an extra gold confetti burst for legendary
  pulls.

Deliberately skipped: real NFTs/blockchain for the memorabilia idea --
serial codes are cosmetic-only strings, not a wallet-backed asset, since
this platform's users are children (COPPA/legal exposure, not to
mention giving kids' in-app items real monetary framing is a bad idea
regardless). Flagged this trade-off in chat with the requester.
